==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|orgueilous}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|orgulous|lang=enm}}, {{term|orgeilous|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|fro}} {{term|orgueilleus|lang=fro}}, {{term|orguillus||proud|lang=fro}}, from {{term|orgoil|lang=fro}}, {{term|orgueil||pride|lang=fro}}, from Old Low {{etyl|frk}} *{{term||urgol|pride|lang=frk}}. Cognate with {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|urguol||excellent|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|orgel||pride|lang=ang}}. More at {{l|en|orgul}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[proud|Proud]]; [[haughty]]; [[disdainful]].
#*  At that time there was a knight, the which was the king's son of Ireland, and his name was Lanceor, the which was an '''orgulous''' knight, and counted himself one of the best of the court; and he had great despite at Balin for the achieving of the sword, that any should be accounted more hardy, or of more prowess. &mdash; Sir Thomas Malory, ''Le Morte D'Arthur'', page 54 (Book II, Chapter IV).
# [[ostentatious|Ostentatious]]; [[showy]].
# [[swollen|Swollen]]; [[augmented]]; [[excessive]]. 
# [[threatening|Threatening]]; [[dangerous]].

